The model predicts one character at a time and keeps adding each new character to the output.
One character is selected, added to the generated text, and then used as part of the input for the next prediction. This process continues repeatedly to generate a longer piece of text.
The demo uses different temperature values: 0.1, 0.5, 0.7, and 1.0. A lower temperature makes the output more predictable because the model mostly chooses the most likely characters.
A higher temperature makes the output more random because the model is more willing to choose less likely characters.
For each temperature, the demo also shows the most likely options for the first next character along with their probabilities, and then generates a longer text output.]


Additional details

For training we will be using below sentences:

"I like cats."
"I like dogs."
"I like noodles."
"I like tacos."
"I like books."
"I like robots."
"I like music."
"I like puzzles."
"I like pizza."
"I like coding."

Join these sentences using a newline between each sentence.

Use a fixed random seed of 1710 so that the results are reproducible. Set the TensorFlow random seed and also create a NumPy random number generator using this seed.

Use a sequence length of 40 characters. Move through the training text one character at a time and use the next character as the target.

Build the model with:
- An Embedding layer with output size 32
- An LSTM layer with 128 units
- A Dense output layer with one output for each unique character

Train the model using:
- Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01
- SparseCategoricalCrossentropy with from_logits=True
- Batch size of 64
- 20 epochs

Before training or generating text, print:
- Corpus length
- Unique characters in the vocabulary
- Number of training samples
- Final training loss

For text generation, start with the seed "I like ". If the seed is shorter than 40 characters, add spaces to the beginning until it reaches 40 characters.

Generate one character at a time. After predicting a character, add it to the output and use it as part of the context for predicting the next character.

Test the temperatures 0.1, 0.5, 0.7, and 1.0.

For each temperature:
- Print the top 5 most likely choices for the first generated character and their probabilities.
- Generate 180 new characters starting from "I like ".

Lower temperatures should make the output more predictable, while higher temperatures should allow more variation.
